<header>

<h1>Math Lab Companion</h1>

<p>
Get the most value from tutoring, office hours, and the math lab.
</p>

</header>

<div class="container">

<h2>Before You Go</h2>

<div class="checklist">

<h3>Bring These 5 Things</h3>

<ul>

<li>✅ Notes</li>

<li>✅ Homework attempts</li>

<li>✅ Calculator</li>

<li>✅ Specific questions</li>

<li>✅ Examples you do not understand</li>

</ul>

</div>

<div class="card">

<h3>Most Important Rule</h3>

<p>

Attempt the problem before asking for help.

</p>

<p>

Tutors can help far more effectively when they can see where your understanding stopped.

</p>

</div>

<h2>Good Questions To Ask</h2>

<div class="question">

<strong>Question #1:</strong>

"Can you check my setup?"

</div>

<div class="question">

<strong>Question #2:</strong>

"Why does this logarithm property apply here?"

</div>

<div class="question">

<strong>Question #3:</strong>

"How do I know when to use logarithms?"

</div>

<div class="question">

<strong>Question #4:</strong>

"Can you show me another example?"

</div>

<div class="question">

<strong>Question #5:</strong>

"How would this appear on an exam?"

</div>

<div class="question">

<strong>Question #6:</strong>

"What mistake am I making repeatedly?"

</div>

<h2>Questions Specific To Chapter 5</h2>

<div class="card">

<ul>

<li>Why is the domain of a logarithm always restricted?</li>

<li>How do I recognize when to use the Product Rule?</li>

<li>How do I know whether to expand or condense?</li>

<li>When should I use logarithms to solve an equation?</li>

<li>Why can't some exponential equations be solved with logs?</li>

<li>How do I find half-life and doubling time?</li>

<li>How do I convert between ab<sup>t</sup> and ae<sup>kt</sup>?</li>

<li>How do I interpret pH and decibel problems?</li>

</ul>

</div>

<h2>If You're Completely Stuck</h2>

<div class="card">

<h3>Tell the Tutor These Three Things</h3>

<ol>

<li>What chapter you're working on.</li>

<li>What you understand so far.</li>

<li>The exact step where you became confused.</li>

</ol>

</div>

<h2>After the Session</h2>

<div class="card">

<h3>Do Not Leave Yet</h3>

<p>

Immediately solve one similar problem without looking at notes.

</p>

<p>

Being able to follow an explanation is not the same thing as being able to solve a problem yourself.

</p>

</div>

<h2>Chapter 5 Reflection Sheet</h2>

<div class="reflection">

<p><strong>What concept feels strongest?</strong></p>

<hr>

<p><strong>What concept still feels confusing?</strong></p>

<hr>

<p><strong>What question do I still have?</strong></p>

<hr>

<p><strong>What problem did I solve successfully today?</strong></p>

<hr>

</div>

<h2>Confidence Strategy</h2>

<div class="card">

<p>

Most students gain confidence in Chapter 5 through:

</p>

<ul>

<li>Repeated practice</li>

<li>Reviewing mistakes</li>

<li>Explaining solutions out loud</li>

<li>Working similar problems</li>

<li>Getting feedback in the Math Lab</li>

</ul>

<p>

Confidence comes from solving problems, not from rereading notes.

</p>

</div>

